contacts with Yugoslavia."},"id":"C1"},{"citation":{"canonical_page_id":"page_5b0e0b869363850aaf2d","corpus":"cib","date":"1957-01-08","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A002900180001-0","earlier_page":4,"end":1603,"page_sha256":"10970f00f89c76ecd5e8d1421f83721c59cb93805b0eab217ccc1de92be52d0a","start":1589,"text":"Polish circles"},"id":"C2"},{"citation":{"canonical_page_id":"page_5b0e0b869363850aaf2d","corpus":"cib","date":"1957-01-08","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A002900180001-0","earlier_page":4,"end":1694,"page_sha256":"10970f00f89c76ecd5e8d1421f83721c59cb93805b0eab217ccc1de92be52d0a","start":1075,"text":"The Hungarian government's pronouncement and the Communist communique followed three days of conversations in Budapest concerning Hungary but including questions dealing with the international situation and the alleged efforts of the West to renew the cold war. This high-level meeting with several Soviet Satellites is a departure from previous post-Stalin practices and is a direct slap at the announced Yugoslav-Polish position that relations between Communist parties should be conducted on a bilateral basis. Polish circles already fear possible Soviet attempts to isolate Warsaw from its contacts with Yugoslavia."},"id":"C3"},{"citation":{"canonical_page_id":"page_5b0e0b869363850aaf2d","corpus":"cib","date":"1957-01-08","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A002900180001-0","earlier_page":4,"end":1588,"page_sha256":"10970f00f89c76ecd5e8d1421f83721c59cb93805b0eab217ccc1de92be52d0a","start":1337,"text":"This high-level meeting with several Soviet Satellites is a departure from previous post-Stalin practices and is a direct slap at the announced Yugoslav-Polish position that relations between Communist parties should be conducted on a bilateral basis."},"id":"C4"},{"citation":{"canonical_page_id":"page_5b0e0b869363850aaf2d","corpus":"cib","date":"1957-01-08","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A002900180001-0","earlier_page":4,"end":1496,"page_sha256":"10970f00f89c76ecd5e8d1421f83721c59cb93805b0eab217ccc1de92be52d0a","start":1481,"text":"Yugoslav-Polish"},"id":"C5"}],"corpus":"cib","date":"1957-01-08","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A002900180001-0","earlier_page":4,"id":"S2","page_sha256":"10970f00f89c76ecd5e8d1421f83721c59cb93805b0eab217ccc1de92be52d0a"},{"canonical_page_id":"page_ca93d75244f2c68be246","citations":[{"citation":{"canonical_page_id":"page_ca93d75244f2c68be246","corpus":"cib","date":"1956-09-22","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A002700380001-0","earlier_page":5,"end":852,"page_sha256":"d9af44b9f2e678ccc8fb5bd866f3d3560a21d224a737447880e3fdd8100d7fe0","start":712,"text":"In any event, Yugoslav officials have encouraged Western diplomats to believe that Soviet-Yugoslav difficulties prompted Khrushchev's visit."},"id":"C1"},{"citation":{"canonical_page_id":"page_ca93d75244f2c68be246","corpus":"cib","date":"1956-09-22","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A002700380001-0","earlier_page":5,"end":744,"page_sha256":"d9af44b9f2e678ccc8fb5bd866f3d3560a21d224a737447880e3fdd8100d7fe0","start":726,"text":"Yugoslav officials"},"id":"C2"},{"citation":{"canonical_page_id":"page_ca93d75244f2c68be246","corpus":"cib","date":"1956-09-22","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A002700380001-0","earlier_page":5,"end":638,"page_sha256":"d9af44b9f2e678ccc8fb5bd866f3d3560a21d224a737447880e3fdd8100d7fe0","start":454,"text":"This Yugoslav hypothesis argues that Khrushchev, having sponsored the rapprochement with Tito, now finds that Tito is endangering the Soviet system by making trouble in the Satellites."},"id":"C3"},{"citation":{"canonical_page_id":"page_ca93d75244f2c68be246","corpus":"cib","date":"1956-09-22","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A002700380001-0","earlier_page":5,"end":467,"page_sha256":"d9af44b9f2e678ccc8fb5bd866f3d3560a21d224a737447880e3fdd8100d7fe0","start":459,"text":"Yugoslav"},"id":"C4"},{"citation":{"canonical_page_id":"page_ca93d75244f2c68be246","corpus":"cib","date":"1956-09-22","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A002700380001-0","earlier_page":5,"end":710,"page_sha256":"d9af44b9f2e678ccc8fb5bd866f3d3560a21d224a737447880e3fdd8100d7fe0","start":454,"text":"This Yugoslav hypothesis argues that Khrushchev, having sponsored the rapprochement with Tito, now finds that Tito is endangering the Soviet system by making trouble in the Satellites. The purpose of Khrushchev's trip is to get Tito to \"call off the dogs.\""},"id":"C5"}],"corpus":"cib","date":"1956-09-22","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A002700380001-0","earlier_page":5,"id":"S3","page_sha256":"d9af44b9f2e678ccc8fb5bd866f3d3560a21d224a737447880e3fdd8100d7fe0"},{"canonical_page_id":"page_17bca7188aa33c1136be","citations":[{"citation":{"canonical_page_id":"page_17bca7188aa33c1136be","corpus":"cib","date":"1957-10-30","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A003300500001-9","earlier_page":7,"end":708,"page_sha256":"fd945c9f9c0684c04e69eae579a4f6e541765a53ed73e9b954593eb7d989d80f","start":519,"text":"Tito's decision not to attend probably reflects his concern over the unsettled situation in Moscow and his desire not to be associated with it until he has some clearer idea of its outcome."},"id":"C1"},{"citation":{"canonical_page_id":"page_17bca7188aa33c1136be","corpus":"cib","date":"1957-10-30","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A003300500001-9","earlier_page":7,"end":525,"page_sha256":"fd945c9f9c0684c04e69eae579a4f6e541765a53ed73e9b954593eb7d989d80f","start":519,"text":"Tito's"},"id":"C2"},{"citation":{"canonical_page_id":"page_17bca7188aa33c1136be","corpus":"cib","date":"1957-10-30","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A003300500001-9","earlier_page":7,"end":1006,"page_sha256":"fd945c9f9c0684c04e69eae579a4f6e541765a53ed73e9b954593eb7d989d80f","start":519,"text":"Tito's decision not to attend probably reflects his concern over the unsettled situation in Moscow and his desire not to be associated with it until he has some clearer idea of its outcome. Tito may have been influenced in this decision by the Yugoslav ambassador in Moscow, with whom Khrushchev reportedly spent more than half his time in earnest conversation at an Iranian embassy reception on 26 October, immediately following the announcement of Marshal Zhukov's removal from office."},"id":"C3"}],"corpus":"cib","date":"1957-10-30","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A003300500001-9","earlier_page":7,"id":"S4","page_sha256":"fd945c9f9c0684c04e69eae579a4f6e541765a53ed73e9b954593eb7d989d80f"},{"canonical_page_id":"page_74f1677739c4be79286d","citations":[{"citation":{"canonical_page_id":"page_74f1677739c4be79286d","corpus":"cib","date":"1956-09-28","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A002700440001-3","earlier_page":5,"end":1271,"page_sha256":"20cbaa577d5e0721c83dcb3924c6b6f26f77f74813ba0b34ca49f36b1449a28a","start":1089,"text":"It is possible that Khrushchev himself may be convinced that Tito is right in his views on Satellite evolution--at least on the necessity for continued far-reaching, gradual changes."},"id":"C1"},{"citation":{"canonical_page_id":"page_74f1677739c4be79286d","corpus":"cib","date":"1956-09-28","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A002700440001-3","earlier_page":5,"end":1154,"page_sha256":"20cbaa577d5e0721c83dcb3924c6b6f26f77f74813ba0b34ca49f36b1449a28a","start":1150,"text":"Tito"},"id":"C2"},{"citation":{"canonical_page_id":"page_74f1677739c4be79286d","corpus":"cib","date":"1956-09-28","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A002700440001-3","earlier_page":5,"end":1431,"page_sha256":"20cbaa577d5e0721c83dcb3924c6b6f26f77f74813ba0b34ca49f36b1449a28a","start":1089,"text":"It is possible that Khrushchev himself may be convinced that Tito is right in his views on Satellite evolution--at least on the necessity for continued far-reaching, gradual changes. In that case, Khrushchev may hope that Tito can convince other Soviet leaders of the necessity of allowing the Satellites to develop more independent policies."},"id":"C3"}],"corpus":"cib","date":"1956-09-28","document_id":"CIA-RDP79T00975A002700440001-3","earlier_page":5,"id":"S5","page_sha256":"20cbaa577d5e0721c83dcb3924c6b6f26f77f74813ba0b34ca49f36b1449a28a"}],"text":"\u201cPolish officials\u2019\u201d is the leading suggestion: the 2 November 1957 report\u2019s heading explicitly identifies Polish officials\u2019 view of Zhukov\u2019s downfall [S1]. This supplies contextual attribution, but does not establish the concealed wording or the proposed link between military resistance to a looser satellite policy and Zhukov\u2019s dismissal. Earlier reporting described Polish circles\u2019 fears of Soviet isolation and the Yugoslav-Polish preference for bilateral party relations [S2]; those concerns illuminate the satellite emphasis without establishing who voiced this particular interpretation.","text_sha256":"ce38fd68b4929bc2e854e24ece8e2a4523a9025b436f4c40ad1dd7ee72d63049","text_utf8_sha256":"1943b9985a73c69feb36314020a8c79f36cd44a85ef3c6c9343b8130a922bb2a","used_for_inference":false,"version":"recovery-study-postrun-digest-overlay/1"}